  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/d6/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_55.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Giant-Man and the Wasp are watching clips of their previous encounter with the Human Top (see [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14]-51) in front of their fan club.&nbsp; They ponder how difficult it was to capture him and express gratitude that he is currently behind bars.\n\nWhat they cannot know is that, miles away, the Human Top is using his spinning powers to escape from prison!&nbsp; After re-donning his uniform, he needs money, so he heads for a bank.&nbsp; Hank and Jan see this reported on the news and immediately spring into action.&nbsp; But they arrive too late to prevent the heist.\n\nBut while Giant-Man and the Wasp are heading home, as fate would have it, the Human Top happens to be heading in the same direction and sees them.&nbsp; When they get home (not realizing they've been followed), they have a normal spat, this time being overheard by the Top outside the window, who realizes how heart-warming it is \"to see two people who are deeply in love that way!\"\n\nHe then swoops in and grabs the belt off of Giant-Man, where he keeps his growth capsules!&nbsp; The Top has found them, and he immediately takes one of the growth capsules and becomes a giant whirlwind.&nbsp; Hank is hurled into a closet, which the Top locks him inside of, and he uses his speed to capture the Wasp inside a small jar.&nbsp; He warns Giant-Man to lay off, takes the Wasp and heads out.\n\nBut Giant-Man still has his cybernetic helmet, which he uses to summon ants, who ultimately bring him a shrinking capsule that allows him to slip  under the door.&nbsp; In moments, he's back in action as Giant-Man, and since the Human Top is still giant-sized himself, it's easy for him to track him down.&nbsp; The two battle briefly before Giant-Man instructs some nearby termites to weaken the roof they're on, and the Top crashes through.\n\nWhile the Top is unconscious, the Wasp pops a shrinking pill into his mouth, and he's recaptured, just as he swears to both of them that the \"next time'll be <i>different</i>!\"\n\nThe newspapers cover the event, and Hank points out Wasp's name in print, leaving Jan to think \"Although I'd rather see it on a <i>wedding license</i>, you big, blind, wonderful goof!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | PlotDevice | width=12%] The Giant-Man and Wasp fan club is a bit goofy, but it was <i>nothing</i> as compared to the Human Top's escape from prison on page 3, where Stan had him \"spinning so fast that he is virtually invisible.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=That's just silly.]&nbsp; Even if you accept this absurd claim, any guard passing by <i>surely</i> would have <b>felt</b> something.&nbsp; After all, there was a guy spinning around inside the cell!&nbsp; A police officer even asks (on the next page), \"Where'd that <i>wind</i> come from?\"&nbsp; And that same page describes his spinning action \"causing a miniature tornado,\" so please don't try and convince me that the prison guard didn't feel anything, because I'm not <i>buying</i> it!\n\nBut of course, the Human Top had already been tagged to be this month's villain, so the story needed a way to get him out of prison.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=This is about the worst case of a 'Hold your nose and move on' plot device that I've seen recently.]&nbsp; The reader is left with no choice but to turn the page with a sinking feeling that this month's adventure might already be doomed.\n\nIt's worth mentioning that on page 2, the Wasp remarks about how \"handsome\" the Human Top is (while reviewing the footage of his capture).&nbsp; This is because, in the <i>film</i>, he removed his helmet and was photographed.&nbsp; But that is never shown in last October's [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09] issue.&nbsp; Its final panel only has the Human Top being led away by police, but his helmet is still on.&nbsp; <i>Yes</i>, it's true that the scene shown in the footage seems to be taken from the other angle, so we can likely assume that his helmet <i>was</i> removed seconds later, thus allowing his actual features to be captured on film.&nbsp; We should also acknowledge the possibility that Jan could also have learned of his true identity during the trial, just before his eventual incarceration.\n\nObviously, none of that matters in the end, as page 18 shows him unmasked again just before he's sent back to prison.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: I only call this out because, given the later stories we read involving Whirlwind (e.g. 1975's Avengers #139), I was under the assumption that Jan did not recognize him, since he was able to pose as her chauffer.&nbsp; I guess we'll just have to keep reading to see how he accomplished this...]\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe last panel of page 5 was unfortunately drawn.\n[ASSET: center | UnfortunatelyDrawn | width=large]\n\nWhen I first looked at it, it appeared that Giant-Man had grown so large as to have to carefully step around cars on the street, and I thought he'd grown taller than ever before, particularly when the Wasp says \"Wait!&nbsp; Stop!&nbsp; You -- you'll <i>kill</i> yourself, Hank!\" (which I thought referred to him growing too tall), but it turns out he was actually throwing himself out the open window many stories up with the intent to grab hold of the tension cable he attached to a reinforced flag pole below.\n\nThis is yet one more example of how Stan (and his artists) so desperately want Hank Pym to be some sort of clever genius, armed with cool toys.&nbsp; Quoting from my own [LINK:1962:TTA_35_ant0|TTA #35] review, I wrote:&nbsp; [SPAN: fonttype=italic; text=he feels a bit more \"pre-fab\" than some of the other heroes competing for readers' spare change, as if reverse engineered from gadgets and not a character who grows into his role.]\n\nThat was over a year-and-a-half ago, even <i>before</i> his funky catapult was ever depicted -- which wasn't until the <i>next </i>issue.\n\nWhat I'm trying to say is that Stan and company are so desperate to position Ant/Giant-Man as some sort of brilliant hero that they never stop to consider the most obvious objection:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=A giant-sized man has just hurled himself out of an upper window, planning to grab a hoop extending from a flagpole below.]\n[ASSET: center | WhatIfHeMisses | width=large]\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=What if he misses?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Is there a plan B?)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sigh...]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Need I mention that the futile race to the bank consumes over three pages of story?&nbsp; We're not <i>getting</i> anywhere here!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | HeartWarming | width=12%] About the only reason their trip to the bank serves from a plot perspective is to put them in a position where the villain can follow them back to their lab, where he overhears their conversation (including Jan calling Hank by name -- so much for any secret identity!).&nbsp; It's so obvious how Hank feels about Jan, that even a <i>few moments</i> of quiet eavesdropping on the part of the Human Top reveals what we readers have known for some time:&nbsp; this is no longer strictly a professional partnership between the two.&nbsp; (Of course, the Wasp herself has never made any attempt to hide her amorous feelings toward Hank.&nbsp; It's just taking Hank a little longer to come to the same conclusion.)\n\n\n[ASSET: right | JarAndLockedCloset | width=12%] After the Human Top grabs Giant-Man's belt, he seems to know about his size-changing pills (which, if it were <i>me</i>, I would never advertise!) -- and even though [LINK:1963:TTA_53_giant0|TTA #53|2027-04-02] showed the Porcupine grabbing capsules from Hank's belt (though ultimately grabbing the wrong ones and shrinking out of sight), at least the Human Top manages to guess <i>correctly</i> (there is a 50% chance of being right after all).\n\n\nAs for being locked in the closet, this has always struck me as very poor -- the idea of putting a lock with a key on a closet door, particularly one that then cannot be opened from the inside!&nbsp; What a horrible design!&nbsp; I get that from the <i>outside</i> you might want to require a key so you can lock the door, preventing access, but what designer in his right mind would not provide an easy way out from the <i>inside</i> in the event that an older sibling (on a bad day) might lock his or her annoying kid brother in the closet!&nbsp; <i>Oh brother!</i>\n\nAnd Jan being trapped in a glass jar?&nbsp; Seriously?&nbsp; Is that all it takes to capture her?&nbsp; Is she really helpless just because she's in a jar?&nbsp; I know she doesn't get strength enhancement until <i>way</i> later (see <i>Marvel Team-Up</i> 59-60), but even a normal sized human might be able to crack a glass jar.&nbsp; I also can't recall if she has a wasp's sting yet, but if she does, that too might be helpful to escape!\n\n\n[ASSET: left | WeakenedRoof | width=19%] I thought the ending was far too convenient.&nbsp; The capture of the villain by weakening the rooftop by summoning <i>termites</i>?&nbsp; One look at the panel that shows how <b>thick</b> this roof actually was seems to make this <i>highly unlikely</i>, not to mention the <i>additional</i> unlikelihood of a building roof being constructed from [SPAN: severity=error; text=nothing but wood!]\n[BR]\n[BR] And don't forget, it's always nice to learn it was done to the roof of a \"deserted tenement.\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(\"The old deserted building trick!\" -- <i>heh!</i>)]\n\n[ASSET: right | WeddingLicense | width=12%] And the gag-worthy ending, once again involving the fan club and reading about their exploits in the newspaper, complete with the Wasp's hope of Hank proposing marriage.\n[BR]\n[BR] All of it only shows how lightweight their latest adventure actually was.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAs empty and devoid of calories as this month's story was, it was astonishingly... passable.&nbsp; The Human Top as depicted here doesn't come across as terribly inventive, but the frightening fact is that he's at <i>least</i> more interesting than the other villains Giant-Man and the Wasp have faced.\n\nThought I admit -- that doesn't actually say much for a title that I'm already on record as saying mostly dread (as it's been a <i>major</i> disappointment thus far), [SPAN: severity=success; text=let's at least give credit where it's due.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=This issue's plot was... <i>okay</i>.]\n\nLet me be very clear though:&nbsp; It won't set the world on fire and likely won't drive readers to clamor for reserve copies, and it absolutely had its share of absurdities, but it mostly went down easy.&nbsp; I was never thrilled, but neither was I ever bored.\n\nHaving the Human Top actually see the Hank/Jan relationship clearly was about the most noteworthy thing in the story.&nbsp; He saw what Hank himself refuses to admit --that the \"professional relationship\" of Giant-Man and the Wasp is anything <i>but</i>.",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"The Gypsy's Secret!\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee, L. D. Lieber and George Bell)\n\nWhen the Wasp interrupts another of Hank's experiments, she declares that he wouldn't be satisfied even if he <i>found</i> what he was looking for, and to illustrate, she begins telling him a tale about an old gypsy named Gorko who uses alchemy in order to turn lead into gold.&nbsp; But even though it could transform his life, he chooses to live humbly and simply in his trailer, all the while searching for <i>something</i> -- in fact, the \"<i>only</i> thing of importance to me!\"\n\nBut one day, his wagon gets stuck in the mud on a road, and he's forced to request assistance at \"a nearby castle\" where a man named Baron Radzik lives.&nbsp; At first, he refuses to help the old man, and when he offers the Baron gold, he simply assumes the gypsy must have <i>stolen</i> it!\n\nBut when the gypsy indicates he can turn lead into gold, the Baron demands he reveal the technique -- the old man <i>refuses</i> to tell one whose heart is filled with such greed.&nbsp; Consequently, the Baron tosses the gypsy into his dungeon until such time as he reveals the secret!\n\nRelying on his wits, the gypsy lies and claims that the technique is too complicated to commit to memory and tells the Baron that he has it written down in his wagon home.&nbsp; The two <i>immediately</i> set out for the wagon.\n\nBut when the Baron enters and finds it dark, he is suddenly trapped by iron bars, and the wagon sheds its outer form and reveals that it is a spaceship, which rockets into the air.&nbsp; To his horror, the Baron learns that the gypsy is <i>really</i> an alien, who is from the \"ninth galaxy,\" sent to Earth to collect a human specimen for an \"intergalactic zoo.\"\n\nBaron Radzik is to be that specimen, for who on Earth would ever miss so greedy a person as he, or \"even shed a tear?\"&nbsp; The alien then assures him that he will indeed share his gold formula, but it will do him little good since gold is a very common metal on his world.\n\nAnd with the moral lesson told (that one can get what one wants and gain no benefit from it), the Wasp concludes.&nbsp; But she learns that her story has only given Hank a <i>new</i> idea:&nbsp; that of discovering the \"ancient formula for turning lead into gold!\"\n\nIt appears there will be no dinner and dancing tonight.&nbsp; She turns sadly away."
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}